comments[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-08-03T20:38:18.653Z","harness:claude-code","Measured the cost of re-orientation on the live tracker 2026-08-03, which is the case this feature exists to serve.\n\nOver a six-hour window, 144 recorded history entries touched 9 distinct items. The surfaces available to a returning caller answer that question three different ways and none of them answers it well.\n\npm context, the command whose entire purpose is orientation, accepts no time window at all: the flag is rejected with exit 2. Its default projection is 8879 bytes and is recomputed in full whether or not anything moved.\n\npm list accepts updated-after and returns 0 rows for that window under its default lifecycle scope, because all 9 changed items are terminal. Raising the scope to all returns the 9. The receipt reports total 0 without disclosing that a filter removed the entire delta.\n\npm activity accepts from and returns the 144 entries, but as operations rather than items: it names ids and ops, so the caller must fold 144 rows back into 9 item states and then read each one.\n\nSo the delta is either unavailable, silently empty, or expressed in a unit the caller cannot act on, and the full projection is the only reliable path. That is the asymmetry this feature closes, and it is worth noting that the unchanged-count in the acceptance criteria is the part that makes the answer trustworthy: without it, a delta of zero rows is indistinguishable from the scoped-away zero measured here.\n"
body: ""
